# The option of this project's own framebuffer (`desktop-cracker/guest-kernel/`).
# It lives here because the module does: `build-kernel.sh` copies this directory into the kernel
# tree and sources this file beside it, so the kernel's own configuration is where the choice is
# made and `make olddefconfig` can resolve it like any other option.
config DESK_FB
	tristate "desktop-cracker's framebuffer (the guest's screen, backed by guest memory)"
	depends on FB
	select FB_SYS_FILLRECT
	select FB_SYS_COPYAREA
	select FB_SYS_IMAGEBLIT
	select FB_SYS_FOPS
	help
	  The framebuffer desktop-cracker's guest draws into when it runs under Firecracker:
	  a real `/dev/fb0` of system memory, with the kernel's own `sys_*` blitters, plus a
	  control node (`/dev/desk_fb-fb`) the guest's agent maps to read the pixels and to be
	  told what the kernel itself drew.

	  Firecracker exposes no display device, so this is where the guest's screen comes from.
	  Say M (or Y) to have a screen at all.
